Let $$L:=A_0(v+w)\oplus A_0(qv)\subseteq V(\omega).$$ Then:
(a) $L$ is a free $A_0$-module of rank $2$ and generates $V(\omega)$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$;
(b) $L$ is not stable under $\tilde e_1$: $\tilde e_1(v+w)=v\notin L$;
(c) $L$ is not weight-decomposed: $L_\omega=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)v=A_0qv$ and $L_{\omega-\alpha_1}=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)w=qA_0w$, but $v+w\notin L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$, so $L\ne L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$.

Consequently there is no set $B$ for which $(L,B)$ is a crystal basis of $V(\omega)$: a crystal basis requires $L$ to be a crystal lattice, i.e. to satisfy [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]] (i)-(iii), and (b) violates (iii) while (c) violates (ii). In particular the axioms (ii) and (iii) are not automatic for a free lattice generating the module. No choice principle is used. The two lattice axioms are independent in the class of allowed graded quantum data. For weight decomposition without operator stability in the same L_1(1), use L'=A0(qv)⊕A0w: ẽw=v∉L'. For operator stability without weight decomposition, take rank-one data P=Z omega⊕Z delta, Pvee=Z h⊕Z d, pairing <h,omega>=<d,delta>=1 and other basis pairings0, alpha=2omega, simple coroot h, d_1=1. On the direct sum of the one-dimensional modules of labels0 and delta all E/F and Kashiwara operators vanish, so A0(v+w)⊕A0(qv) is stable under them but is not label-weight decomposed. Thus neither axiom follows from the other for general crystal lattices. These two additional explicit witnesses justify the original independence clause; the first mixed lattice alone did not.

Let $(L_1,B_1),(L_2,B_2)$ be crystal bases of integrable modules as in [[thm-crystal-basis-tensor-product-rule]] and let $B_1\otimes B_2$ be the tensor product crystal of [[def-tensor-product-of-crystals]] with the two-case operators of that theorem. Then for all $b_1\in B_1$, $b_2\in B_2$ and $i\in I$:

(a) $wt(b_1\otimes b_2)=wt(b_1)+wt(b_2)$;

(b) $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\max\bigl(\varepsilon_i(b_1),\ \varepsilon_i(b_2)-\langle h_i,wt(b_1)\rangle\bigr)$;

(c) $\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\max\bigl(\varphi_i(b_1)+\langle h_i,wt(b_2)\rangle,\ \varphi_i(b_2)\bigr)$;

(d) the two cases are exactly the alternatives in the max formulas: $\tilde f_i$ acts on the first factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)>\varepsilon_i(b_2)$, and then $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\varepsilon_i(b_1)$ (this equality also holds at the tie); it acts on the second factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)\le\varepsilon_i(b_2)$, and then $\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)=\varepsilon_i(b_2)-\langle h_i,wt(b_1)\rangle$, and dually $\tilde e_i$ acts on the first factor if and only if $\varphi_i(b_1)\ge\varepsilon_i(b_2)$;

(e) **partial-inverse property.** For all $b,b'\in B_1\otimes B_2$ and every $i$, $\tilde f_ib=b'$ if and only if $b=\tilde e_ib'$; in particular the assignment of the non-strict comparison to $\tilde e_i$ and the strict comparison to $\tilde f_i$ is the one for which the two-case rule is internally consistent;

(f) along the $i$-string, $\varepsilon_i(\tilde f_i^{\,n}(b_1\otimes b_2))=\varepsilon_i(b_1\otimes b_2)+n$ and $\varphi_i(\tilde f_i^{\,n}(b_1\otimes b_2))=\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)-n$ for $0\le n\le\varphi_i(b_1\otimes b_2)$.

No choice principle is used.

Let $\lambda\in P^+$ and let $B(\lambda)=\mathcal B(\lambda)$ be the crystal of $V(\lambda)=L(\lambda)$ from [[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], which is a crystal basis by [[thm-grand-loop-existence-of-highest-weight-crystal-bases]]. Then $B(\lambda)$ is a highest weight crystal of highest weight $\lambda$ in the sense of [[def-highest-weight-element-and-connected-highest-weight-crystal]]:

(i) $b_\lambda:=v_\lambda+q\mathcal L(\lambda)$ is a highest weight element of weight $\lambda$: $wt(b_\lambda)=\lambda$ and $\tilde e_ib_\lambda=0$ for every $i\in I$;

(ii) $B(\lambda)=F(b_\lambda)$: every $b\in\mathcal B(\lambda)$ is an iterated lowering image $b=\tilde f_{i_1}\cdots\tilde f_{i_r}v_\lambda$;

(iii) $b_\lambda$ is the only highest weight element of $B(\lambda)$: $\mathcal B(\lambda)_\lambda=\{b_\lambda\}$, and for every $\mu\ne\lambda$ and every nonzero homogeneous $u\in\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu/q\mathcal L(\lambda)_\mu$ there is $i\in I$ with $\tilde e_iu\ne0$.

No choice principle is used.

